<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  The sentences macOS reads out on SPAWN D's behalf.

  `spawnd` is a bare Mach-O, not an app bundle, so it has nowhere to keep an
  Info.plist unless one is linked into it: build.rs stamps this file into the
  `__TEXT,__info_plist` section of both binaries. Without it every consent
  dialog on the machine is a lower-case path — "spawnd" would like to access
  your Desktop folder — with no icon, no product, and no reason given, which is
  precisely the dialog nobody should ever grant.

  Every `NS*UsageDescription` below is the second line of a real dialog. macOS
  shows them verbatim and truncates long ones, so they are one sentence, say
  who is asking and what for, and are true of what the daemon actually does.
  There is no key for `kTCCServiceSystemPolicyAppData` — Apple ships none — so
  that one dialog stays wordless whatever we write here.

  CFBundleIdentifier is load-bearing twice over: codesign adopts it as the
  signing identifier, so the daemon stops identifying itself as a per-build
  hash like `spawnd-fc29710558bd7071`, and TCC keys a Developer ID grant on it,
  so a grant given once survives the next self-update instead of asking again.
  Both binaries deliberately carry the *same* identifier: `spawn-worker` can end
  up its own responsible process, and the person allowed SPAWN D once — not a
  supervisor and a worker separately.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>CFBundleName</key>
	<string>SPAWN D</string>
	<key>CFBundleDisplayName</key>
	<string>SPAWN D</string>
	<key>CFBundleIdentifier</key>
	<string>dev.spawnd.daemon</string>
	<key>CFBundlePackageType</key>
	<string>APPL</string>
	<key>CFBundleShortVersionString</key>
	<string>__SPAWND_VERSION__</string>
	<key>CFBundleVersion</key>
	<string>__SPAWND_VERSION__</string>

	<key>NSDesktopFolderUsageDescription</key>
	<string>SPAWN D reads and writes files on your Desktop when you open them from SPAWN D, or when an agent you started works there.</string>
	<key>NSDocumentsFolderUsageDescription</key>
	<string>SPAWN D reads and writes files in Documents when you open them from SPAWN D, or when an agent you started works there.</string>
	<key>NSDownloadsFolderUsageDescription</key>
	<string>SPAWN D reads and writes files in Downloads when you open them from SPAWN D, or when an agent you started works there.</string>
	<key>NSFileProviderDomainUsageDescription</key>
	<string>SPAWN D reads files kept in iCloud Drive and other file providers when you open them from SPAWN D, or an agent you started works there.</string>
	<key>NSNetworkVolumesUsageDescription</key>
	<string>SPAWN D reads files on network volumes when you open them from SPAWN D, or an agent you started works there.</string>
	<key>NSRemovableVolumesUsageDescription</key>
	<string>SPAWN D reads files on drives you connect when you open them from SPAWN D, or an agent you started works there.</string>
	<key>NSPhotoLibraryUsageDescription</key>
	<string>SPAWN D can open one file inside your photo library if you browse to it. It never reads the library otherwise, and SPAWN D never asks for this on its own.</string>
	<key>NSAppleMusicUsageDescription</key>
	<string>SPAWN D can open one file inside your music library if you browse to it. It never reads the library otherwise, and SPAWN D never asks for this on its own.</string>
</dict>
</plist>
